    "body": "Drilling at the Haib project site. Credit: Koryx Copper.\nPosted By: Henry Lazenby November 18, 2026\nKoryx Copper (TSXV: KRY; NSX: KYX; US-OTC: KRYXF) is proposing a bigger mine at its Haib project in Namibia, where a new study raises the upfront cost by 32% and planned annual payable copper output by 41% for the first decade.\nThe first prefeasibility study (PFS) puts the cost of building Haib at $2.06 billion (C$2.94 billion), up from $1.56 billion in the September 2025 assessment. The open pit would yield 130,000 tonnes of payable copper a year over its first decade, up from 92,000 tonnes. Haib is about 780 km by road south of Windhoek, near the South African border.\nA simpler plant and less waste rock to move make the study “a clear de-risking step,” Red Cloud Securities mining analyst Ian Parkinson wrote Wednesday. “In our view, a stressed Orange River remains the main risk,” he added.\nFirst reserve\nThe study gives Koryx its first mineral reserve at Haib and a mine plan to take to potential partners. Koryx estimated its cash at C$35 million at the end of October in an investor presentation. It is seeking financing while it prepares a definitive feasibility study.\n“The PFS cements our view that Haib is a buildable large-scale project,” Haywood Securities mining analyst Jamie Spratt wrote Tuesday, citing its infrastructure and location. “We expect this will make the full suite of strategic options available to management” as it pursues financing and permits, he added.\nKoryx shares fell 7.8% to C$3.18 at Tuesday’s close in Toronto, valuing the company at about C$390 million. Spratt kept his buy rating and C$8 target. Parkinson kept his buy rating and raised his target to C$6.50 from C$5. Red Cloud disclosed it had provided investment banking services to Koryx in the past year.\nThe upfront cost exceeded BMO Capital Markets mining analyst Rene Cartier’s $1.7-billion estimate. But stronger-than-expected production in the early years would help Koryx recover its investment sooner, he wrote Tuesday. Life-of-project cash costs of $2.19 per lb. also came in below his $2.66 estimate. Cartier raised his target to C$6.50.\nHigher grades first\nAt a copper price of $5 per lb., Haib has an after-tax net present value of $2.54 billion at an 8% discount rate, and a 25% internal rate of return, Koryx said. The September 2025 assessment put the value at $1.35 billion and the return at 20%, based on $4.31 per pound.\nThe revised plan would move 0.98 tonne of waste per tonne of ore over the life of the project, down from 1.88 tonnes in the earlier study. Koryx also dropped heap leaching. Its redesigned plant would discard about a quarter of the crushed rock before final flotation. The mine would send higher-grade material to the mill first and save lower-grade rock for the final nine years, when the plant would run on stockpiles. Over 24 years, Haib would average 105,000 tonnes of payable copper, 12,000 oz. of payable gold and 1.13 million lb. of payable molybdenum a year.\nThe Nov. 6 estimate puts Haib’s probable reserve at 1.12 billion tonnes at 0.27% copper, 58 parts per million molybdenum and 0.02 g/t gold. The reserve contains about 3 million tonnes of copper, 65,000 tonnes of molybdenum and 689,000 oz. of gold.\nPermitting\nKoryx applied for a mining licence in September 2025 and submitted its mine environmental assessment in August 2026. The project would need about 20 million cubic metres of water a year. Koryx plans to draw from the Orange River only when flows exceed the needs of downstream users and the environment, and store the surplus behind an off-channel dam. The study budgets $80 million for the water supply works and $26 million for the dam.\nThe operation would use grid power supplemented by on-site solar and battery storage. Koryx aims to secure major permits around mid-2027 while it works on a definitive feasibility study. CEO Heye Daun is targeting early works late next year and construction in the first quarter of 2028.\nPrint\nCOMMODITY: Copper\nREGION: Africa Canada\nRepublish this article",
